# G112 — DB Translator (dialect-specific DDL)

*v2. See `db-translator.md` for prose: one abstract type, many concrete DDL
strings; dialect as bare-word at render time, not bake-in.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Schema Migrator

[schema-migrator @erd @target-connection
    IN ORDER TO
        let the user pick a SQL dialect
        preview the generated DDL
        and execute it on the target database only on confirmation

    @tables <- [erd[to-translator-tables {erd: @erd}]]

    sync @user-events {
        pick-target @dialect ->
            @ddl <- [dbt[emit-schema {tables: @tables, dialect: @dialect}]]
            [ui[preview-ddl {text: @ddl}]]

            <- @user{confirm DDL execution}
            [db[execute-script {connection: @target-connection, script: @ddl}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[dbt
    type-small-int
    type-integer
    type-varchar        {length}
    type-boolean
    type-timestamp
    default-literal     {str}
    default-now
    column              {name, ty}
    table               {name, columns}
    render-type         {type, dialect}
    render-default      {default, dialect}
    emit-create-table   {table, dialect}
    emit-schema         {tables, dialect}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: dialect as late-binding bare-word

[dbt[render-type {type: @ty, dialect: @d}
    where
        for "postgres"  type-small-int renders "SMALLINT",
                        type-varchar(n) renders "VARCHAR(n)",
                        type-boolean renders "BOOLEAN",
                        type-timestamp renders "TIMESTAMPTZ"
        and for "mysql" type-boolean renders "TINYINT(1)",
                        type-timestamp renders "DATETIME"
        and for "sqlite" type-boolean renders "INTEGER",
                         type-timestamp renders "TEXT"
]

One table definition; three target strings. The `where` clause
documents the translation table; adding a new dialect is a resolver
extension.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @t <- [dbt[table {name: "user",
                       columns: [[dbt[column {name: "id",       ty: [dbt[type-integer]]}]]
                                  [dbt[column {name: "name",     ty: [dbt[type-varchar {length: 100}]]}]]
                                  [dbt[column {name: "active",   ty: [dbt[type-boolean]]}]]
                                  [dbt[column {name: "created",  ty: [dbt[type-timestamp]]}]]]}]]

    -> [dbt[emit-create-table {table: @t, dialect: "postgres"}]]
    ;; -> "CREATE TABLE user (...BOOLEAN...TIMESTAMPTZ)"

    -> [dbt[emit-create-table {table: @t, dialect: "mysql"}]]
    ;; -> "CREATE TABLE user (...TINYINT(1)...DATETIME)"
]
